\lessonlabel{1}
\section{What Accounting Is}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the purpose of accounting, break it into its essential functions and explain why each function matters;
  \item identify the main categories of users of financial information, describe the specific information each category seeks, and recognise common misconceptions about user needs;
  \item write the accounting equation, define assets, liabilities and equity in detail, and demonstrate step‑by‑step how the equation stays balanced after different types of transactions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Purpose of accounting}

\term{Accounting} is the systematic process of recording, classifying, summarising, and interpreting the financial transactions of an entity.  The purpose can be described in three inter‑related layers.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Information provision.}  Accounting creates a permanent, verifiable record of every economic event that affects the entity.  This record is the raw material for the profit‑and‑loss statement, the balance sheet, and the cash‑flow statement.  Without a reliable record, any later analysis would be based on guesswork.
  \item \textbf{Decision support.}  Managers, owners and external parties use the reports to evaluate past performance, assess current risk and plan future actions.  For example, a manager may compare the cost of two suppliers, while an investor may examine the trend in net income to decide whether to buy more shares.
  \item \textbf{Accountability and control.}  By documenting every inflow and outflow, accounting provides evidence that resources are being used as intended and that legal or contractual obligations are met.  Auditors and regulators rely on this evidence to confirm compliance.
\end{enumerate}

A frequent mistake is to equate accounting with bookkeeping.  Bookkeeping is the mechanical act of entering numbers into journals; accounting adds classification, summarisation and interpretation, turning raw data into knowledge that can be acted upon.

\begin{example}
A small bakery records each cash sale, each purchase of flour, and each wage payment in a daily journal.  At the end of the month the accountant groups sales by product, deducts the cost of flour and wages, and prepares a profit‑and‑loss statement.  The owner sees that croissant sales generate a higher margin than bread, and decides to allocate more oven time to croissants.  The same data also appear on the balance sheet, showing that the bakery holds \$12,000 in cash and \$5,000 in inventory, which helps the owner plan cash needs for the next month.
\end{example}

\subsection{Users of financial information}

Financial information is prepared for a variety of external and internal users.  Each group looks for different pieces of the same reports, and confusing these needs can lead to mis‑interpretation.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Owners and investors} -- need to know whether the business is generating a return on the capital they have provided.  They focus on net income, earnings per share, dividend potential and the growth of equity.
  \item \term{Creditors and lenders} -- examine the ability of the business to repay existing and future debt.  Key items for them are current assets, current liabilities, interest‑coverage ratios and cash‑flow from operations.
  \item \term{Management} -- uses detailed internal reports, often more frequent than external statements, to control costs, set prices, allocate resources and compare actual results with budgets.
  \item \term{Government and regulators} -- require accurate taxable profit, compliance with accounting standards and evidence of lawful operation.  They rely on the profit‑and‑loss statement for tax calculation and on the balance sheet for statutory disclosures.
  \item \term{Employees and unions} -- are interested in the firm's stability and its capacity to pay wages, bonuses or profit‑sharing.  They look at overall profitability, cash reserves and long‑term solvency.
\end{itemize}

A common error is to assume that a single financial statement satisfies all users.  In practice, each group extracts different ratios, trends or sections of the statements to meet its specific decision‑making needs.

\begin{example}
A venture‑capital firm is considering an investment in a tech start‑up.  It studies the start‑up's income statement to gauge profitability, the balance sheet to assess the level of debt versus equity, and the cash‑flow statement to verify that the reported profits are supported by actual cash generation.  Only when all three perspectives are satisfactory does the venture‑capital firm proceed with the investment.
\end{example}

\subsection{The accounting equation}

The logical backbone of all double‑entry accounting is the \term{accounting equation}:

\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]

\textbf{Definitions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Assets} -- resources owned or controlled by the entity that are expected to provide future economic benefits.  Examples include cash, accounts receivable, inventory, equipment and patents.
  \item \textbf{Liabilities} -- present obligations to outsiders that will require an outflow of resources.  Examples are bank loans, accounts payable, accrued wages and tax liabilities.
  \item \textbf{Equity} -- the residual interest of the owners after all liabilities have been deducted.  It represents the owners' claim on the assets and includes share capital, retained earnings and other reserves.
\end{itemize}

The equation must remain in balance after every transaction because each economic event either (a) adds a resource, (b) creates a claim on a resource, or (c) transfers ownership between the three categories.  The double‑entry system guarantees this balance by requiring that total debits equal total credits.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step reasoning for a typical transaction}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify all accounts that the transaction touches.
  \item Decide for each account whether it increases (debit for assets, credit for liabilities and equity) or decreases (credit for assets, debit for liabilities and equity).
  \item Record the amounts as equal debits and credits; the sum of the changes to assets will always equal the sum of the changes to liabilities plus equity.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake.}  Recording only one side of a transaction---such as increasing cash without recording the corresponding increase in a liability or equity---creates an imbalance that will be revealed when the trial balance is prepared.

\begin{example}
A consulting firm receives \$8,000 in cash for services that have already been performed.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cash (asset) increases by \$8,000 -- debit.
  \item Service revenue (part of equity) increases by \$8,000 -- credit.
\end{itemize}
Assets rise by \$8,000, equity rises by the same amount, and the equation stays balanced.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
The same firm purchases a laptop for \$2,000, paying \$1,200 in cash and borrowing the remaining \$800 on a short‑term loan.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Laptop (asset) increases by \$2,000 -- debit.
  \item Cash (asset) decreases by \$1,200 -- credit.
  \item Short‑term loan (liability) increases by \$800 -- credit.
\end{itemize}
Total assets increase by \$800, liabilities increase by \$800, and equity is unchanged, preserving the equation.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
A corporation issues new shares and receives \$20,000 cash from investors.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cash (asset) increases by \$20,000 -- debit.
  \item Share capital (equity) increases by \$20,000 -- credit.
\end{itemize}
Only the asset and equity sides change, and the increase is equal, so the equation remains in balance.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.}  After posting any transaction, add the monetary values of all asset accounts and compare the total to the sum of all liability and equity accounts.  The two totals must be identical; any difference signals an error in the double‑entry recording.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Explain in one sentence why providing reliable profit information is a core purpose of accounting.
  \item List three different users of financial statements and state one specific piece of information each user typically requires.
  \item A company receives \$5,000 cash from a customer for services to be performed next month. Show how this transaction affects the accounting equation.
  \item The same company later performs the service and records the revenue. Show the impact on assets, liabilities and equity.
  \item A firm pays \$1,200 cash to settle part of a bank loan. Record the effect on each side of the equation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.5cm}p{11.5cm}@{}}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
Accounting & Systematic recording, classifying, summarising and interpreting of financial transactions. \\
Purpose of accounting & To provide reliable information, to support decision‑making, and to ensure accountability and control of resources. \\
Users of financial information & Groups that rely on accounting reports, such as owners, creditors, management, government and employees. \\
Assets & Economic resources owned or controlled by an entity that are expected to generate future benefits. \\
Liabilities & Present obligations that will require an outflow of resources to settle. \\
Equity & Residual interest of owners after deducting liabilities from assets; includes share capital and retained earnings. \\
Accounting equation & The identity Assets = Liabilities + Equity that must hold after every transaction. \\
Double‑entry & The rule that every transaction is recorded with equal total debits and credits, guaranteeing the accounting equation stays balanced. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Recording Transactions}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item recognise and describe the main types of source documents used in business accounting;
  \item explain why double‑entry bookkeeping is required and how it keeps the accounting equation in balance;
  \item define \term{debit} and \term{credit}, state how each affects the five major account types, and apply the rules to a range of typical transactions;
  \item avoid common mistakes such as reversing debits and credits or omitting a second entry.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Source documents}

\term{Source documents} are the original pieces of evidence that a transaction has taken place. They may be paper‑based (invoices, receipts) or electronic (e‑mails, PDFs). A reliable accounting system never records a transaction without first locating a source document, because the document provides the audit trail required for verification and for external review.

Typical source documents include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Sales invoice}: issued by a seller to a buyer; shows date, buyer name, items sold, unit price, total amount, tax, and payment terms.
  \item \textbf{Purchase receipt}: issued by a supplier; records date, supplier name, goods or services received, amount paid, and method of payment.
  \item \textbf{Bank statement}: monthly summary from a bank; lists all deposits, withdrawals, fees, and interest for the period.
  \item \textbf{Cash register tape}: printed record from a point‑of‑sale system; details each cash sale, time, and amount.
  \item \textbf{Payroll slip}: prepared by the payroll department; shows employee name, pay period, gross wages, deductions, and net pay.
\end{itemize}

Each document must contain at least four pieces of information:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Date} -- when the transaction occurred;
  \item \textbf{Parties} -- who gave and who received value;
  \item \textbf{Amount} -- monetary value, often broken into sub‑totals;
  \item \textbf{Description} -- brief narrative of the transaction.
\end{enumerate}
The accountant extracts these data points and enters them into the ledger or accounting software. Missing or incomplete source documents are a common source of error; always verify that the document is authentic and complete before posting.

\begin{example}
A catering company receives a delivery of fresh produce. The delivery note (a source document) reads:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Date: 5 April 2026
  \item Supplier: Green Farms Ltd.
  \item Items: 20 kg tomatoes, 15 kg lettuce, 10 kg carrots
  \item Unit price: \$2 per kg (tomatoes), \$1.5 per kg (lettuce), \$1 per kg (carrots)
  \item Total amount: \$85
\end{itemize}
The accountant records a purchase of inventory worth \$85, using the delivery note as proof of receipt.
\end{example}

\subsection{Double‑entry bookkeeping}

\term{Double‑entry bookkeeping} is the foundation of modern accounting. The principle states that every economic event must be recorded in at least two accounts: one account receives a \term{debit} entry, another receives a \term{credit} entry. This ensures that the fundamental accounting equation remains true after each posting:
\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]

Why two entries? When a company acquires an asset, it must give up something of equal value---cash, a liability, or equity. Recording both sides makes the flow of resources transparent and provides built‑in error checking: if total debits do not equal total credits, the transaction is incomplete or incorrectly posted.

The process for a new transaction is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the economic event and locate the source document.
  \item Determine which accounts are affected (e.g., cash, inventory, revenue).
  \item Decide which account is increased and which is decreased, using the debit/credit rules.
  \item Write the journal entry, listing the debit first, then the credit, with equal monetary amounts.
  \item Post the amounts to the respective ledger accounts.
\end{enumerate}

Common mistakes at this stage include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Omitting the second entry}: posting only a debit or only a credit breaks the equation.
  \item \textbf{Reversing the direction}: debiting an account that should be credited (or vice‑versa) leads to misstated balances.
  \item \textbf{Using the wrong account type}: for example, recording a cash receipt as a debit to \textit{Accounts Payable} instead of \textit{Cash}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A consulting firm receives a \$2,500 cash payment for services rendered. The journal entry is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Debit \textit{Cash} \$2,500 (asset increase)
  \item Credit \textit{Service Revenue} \$2,500 (equity increase)
\end{itemize}
Debits equal credits, and the equation stays balanced: assets rise by \$2,500, equity rises by the same amount.
\end{example}

\subsection{Debits and credits}

The terms \term{debit} (Dr) and \term{credit} (Cr) describe the two sides of every account. Their effect depends on the account classification. The five major account types are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Assets}: resources owned (cash, inventory, equipment).
  \item \textbf{Liabilities}: obligations to outsiders (accounts payable, loans).
  \item \textbf{Equity}: owners' residual interest (capital, retained earnings).
  \item \textbf{Revenue}: inflows from ordinary activities (sales, fees).
  \item \textbf{Expenses}: outflows incurred to earn revenue (rent, utilities).
\end{itemize}

The debit/credit rules are:

\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
Account type & Debit effect & Credit effect \\
\hline
Assets & increase & decrease \\
Liabilities & decrease & increase \\
Equity & decrease & increase \\
Revenue & decrease & increase \\
Expenses & increase & decrease \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

To apply the rules, follow these steps for each transaction:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item List all accounts involved.
  \item Classify each account (asset, liability, etc.).
  \item Decide whether the account is increasing or decreasing.
  \item Apply the appropriate debit or credit based on the table above.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
The firm purchases a computer on credit for \$1,200.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Computer Equipment} (asset) increases $\rightarrow$ Debit \$1,200.
  \item \textit{Accounts Payable} (liability) increases $\rightarrow$ Credit \$1,200.
\end{itemize}
The entry balances and reflects that the firm now owes the supplier while gaining an asset.
\end{example}

A frequent error is to treat ``credit'' as a synonym for ``negative'' in all contexts. In reality, a credit to an asset account reduces the asset, while a credit to a liability or equity account increases it. Remember to consult the rule table rather than rely on intuition.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Record the following transaction using double‑entry bookkeeping: The firm pays \$350 cash for a monthly internet service bill.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify three source documents that would support a cash purchase of office furniture and list the four required data items each must contain.
  \item For each scenario, write the correct debit and credit accounts with amounts:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item The company sells services on account for \$4,200.
      \item The company receives a \$5,000 bank loan and deposits the cash in the bank.
      \item The company pays \$600 cash for advertising expenses.
    \end{enumerate}
  \item Explain, in your own words, why the double‑entry system prevents the accounting equation from becoming unbalanced.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{|p{3.5cm}|p{10.5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
Source document & Original paper or electronic record that proves a transaction occurred; contains date, parties, amount, and description. \\
\hline
Double‑entry bookkeeping & System requiring at least two accounts per transaction, with equal debits and credits, to keep the accounting equation balanced. \\
\hline
Debit (Dr) & Left‑hand entry; increases assets and expenses, decreases liabilities, equity, and revenue. \\
\hline
Credit (Cr) & Right‑hand entry; increases liabilities, equity, and revenue, decreases assets and expenses. \\
\hline
Asset & Resource owned or controlled by the entity that is expected to provide future economic benefit. \\
\hline
Liability & Present obligation arising from past events, settlement of which is expected to result in an outflow of resources. \\
\hline
Equity & Owner's residual interest after liabilities are deducted from assets. \\
\hline
Revenue & Inflows from the ordinary activities of the entity that increase equity. \\
\hline
Expense & Outflows or using up of assets that decrease equity. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{The General Ledger}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define a \term{ledger account}, describe its physical or electronic layout, and explain why it is a permanent record;
  \item list each step of the \term{posting} process, apply it to typical transactions, and recognise the most common posting errors;
  \item calculate the balance of any ledger account, state whether it is a debit or credit balance, and explain how the balance is carried forward to the next period;
  \item compare the general ledger with subsidiary records such as journals and subsidiary ledgers, and relate the ledger to the trial balance and the financial statements.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Ledger accounts}

A \term{ledger account} is a dedicated page in a physical ledger book or a separate file in accounting software that collects every debit and credit entry that relates to one specific element of the accounting equation. The element can be an asset (Cash, Inventory), a liability (Accounts Payable), equity (Owner's Capital), a revenue (Service Revenue) or an expense (Rent Expense).  

The account is drawn as a two‑column ``T‑account''. The left side is called the \term{debit side}; the right side is the \term{credit side}. Each line in the account records four pieces of information:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Date}: the day the transaction occurred.
  \item \textbf{Reference}: usually the journal page number, a transaction code, or a document number that links back to the source.
  \item \textbf{Description}: a brief note of the source document (e.g., ``Invoice 1023'').
  \item \textbf{Amount}: placed in the debit column or the credit column according to the normal balance rule for that account type.
\end{itemize}

The \term{normal balance rule} states that assets and expenses normally carry a debit balance, while liabilities, equity and revenues normally carry a credit balance. This rule tells you on which side to record a new amount.  

A ledger account serves two main purposes. First, it shows the chronological development of a single item, making it easy to trace the effect of each transaction. Second, the final balance of the account becomes a line in the trial balance, which is the basis for the income statement and balance sheet.

\textbf{Common mistake.} Beginners often reverse the sides: recording a cash receipt as a credit in the Cash account. Because Cash is an asset, the correct entry is a debit. The reversal creates a false credit balance, which later prevents the trial balance from balancing.

\begin{example}
\textbf{Cash account -- extended activity}\\
Opening balance: \$0.  

\begin{itemize}
  \item 02 Jan: cash sale of \$750 -- entered as a debit of \$750 on the left.
  \item 10 Jan: paid electricity bill of \$180 -- entered as a credit of \$180 on the right.
  \item 15 Jan: received a loan of \$1{,}200 -- entered as a debit of \$1{,}200 on the left.
  \item 22 Jan: purchased office supplies for cash \$320 -- entered as a credit of \$320 on the right.
  \item 30 Jan: customer paid outstanding account receivable \$500 -- entered as a debit of \$500 on the left.
\end{itemize}

Totals: Debit \$2{,}550, Credit \$500. Net balance = \$2{,}050 debit, written on the left as ``Balance c/f \$2{,}050''. The running total after each entry helps detect errors early.
\end{example}

\subsection{Posting transactions}

\term{Posting} is the systematic transfer of each amount from a journal entry to the appropriate ledger accounts. The journal records the transaction once, while the ledger records each component of the transaction in the relevant permanent accounts.

The posting process must respect the double‑entry rule: total debits must equal total credits. The steps are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Read the journal entry}. Identify every account named.
  \item \textbf{Determine the normal side}. For each account, decide whether the amount is a debit or a credit based on its type.
  \item \textbf{Open the ledger page}. Locate the T‑account (or electronic ledger) for each account.
  \item \textbf{Enter date, reference and description}. This creates an audit trail back to the source document.
  \item \textbf{Write the amount in the correct column}. Debit amounts go on the left, credit amounts on the right.
  \item \textbf{Update running totals}. Add the new amount to the cumulative debit or credit total for that account.
  \item \textbf{Cross‑check}. Verify that the sum of debits posted equals the sum of credits posted for the whole journal entry.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Typical errors.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Side reversal}: posting a debit amount to the credit column or vice‑versa.
  \item \emph{Missing reference}: omitting the journal page number makes later verification difficult.
  \item \emph{Incomplete posting}: forgetting to post to one of the accounts leaves the trial balance out of balance.
  \item \emph{Transposition of figures}: writing \$1{,}200 as \$2{,}100 changes the financial picture.
  \item \emph{Duplicate posting}: entering the same amount twice inflates the balance.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
\textbf{Purchase of inventory on credit}\\
Journal entry (20 Feb):
\[
\text{Inventory}\ \text{Debit}\ \$3{,}500\\
\text{Accounts Payable}\ \text{Credit}\ \$3{,}500
\]

Posting steps:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify accounts: Inventory (asset) and Accounts Payable (liability).
  \item Debit Inventory: write \$3{,}500 on the left side of the Inventory ledger, date 20 Feb, reference J‑22.
  \item Credit Accounts Payable: write \$3{,}500 on the right side of the Accounts Payable ledger, same date and reference.
  \item Update totals: Inventory now has a debit total of \$3{,}500; Accounts Payable has a credit total of \$3{,}500.
  \item Cross‑check: total debits (\$3{,}500) equal total credits (\$3{,}500).
\end{enumerate}
The two ledger entries mirror the journal entry, preserving the equality of debits and credits.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
\textbf{Partial payment of a liability}\\
Journal entry (05 Mar):
\[
\text{Accounts Payable}\ \text{Debit}\ \$1{,}200\\
\text{Cash}\ \text{Credit}\ \$1{,}200
\]

Posting:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Accounts involved: Accounts Payable (liability) and Cash (asset).
  \item Debit Accounts Payable: place \$1{,}200 on the left side, reducing the liability.
  \item Credit Cash: place \$1{,}200 on the right side, reducing the asset.
  \item Record date 05 Mar and reference J‑35 in both ledgers.
  \item Update totals and cross‑check that debits equal credits.
\end{enumerate}
After posting, the Accounts Payable balance falls by \$1{,}200, and the Cash balance falls by the same amount, keeping the trial balance in equilibrium.
\end{example}

\subsection{Balancing an account}

Balancing a ledger account means determining the net amount that remains after all debits and credits for a period have been recorded. The balance is always shown on the side with the larger total, and it is labelled ``Balance c/f'' (carried forward) for the next period.

Mathematically:
\[
\text{Balance}=
\begin{cases}
\text{Total Debits}-\text{Total Credits}, & \text{if Debits}>\text{Credits (debit balance)}\\[4pt]
\text{Total Credits}-\text{Total Debits}, & \text{if Credits}>\text{Debits (credit balance)}
\end{cases}
\]

\textbf{Balancing steps}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Add all debit entries in the account.
  \item Add all credit entries in the account.
  \item Compare the two sums.
  \item Write the difference on the side with the larger total and label it ``Balance c/f''.
  \item Transfer this balance to the trial balance column.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake.} Some students subtract the larger total from the smaller one, producing a negative figure and placing it on the wrong side. The correct method always subtracts the smaller total from the larger total, then records the result on the side of the larger total.

\begin{example}
\textbf{Supplies account balance}\\
Entries recorded during the month:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Debit entries: \$1{,}200 (purchase), \$300 (additional purchase) -- total \$1{,}500.
  \item Credit entries: \$400 (used in production) -- total \$400.
\end{itemize}
Because \$1{,}500 > \$400, the balance is a debit of \$1{,}100 (\$1{,}500-\$400). The closing line is written on the left as ``Balance c/f \$1{,}100'' and will be carried forward to the next month.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
\textbf{Accounts Payable balance after two transactions}\\
During the quarter:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Credit entries: \$5{,}000 (purchase on credit), \$2{,}000 (additional purchase) -- total \$7{,}000.
  \item Debit entries: \$3{,}200 (partial payment), \$1{,}000 (cash discount received) -- total \$4{,}200.
\end{itemize}
Credits exceed debits, so the account carries a credit balance of \$2{,}800 (\$7{,}000-\$4{,}200). The closing line reads ``Balance c/f \$2{,}800'' on the right side.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Perform the complete posting process for the following journal entry and then balance each affected ledger account.

Journal entry (date 12 Apr):
\[
\text{Equipment}\ \text{Debit}\ \$4{,}800\\
\text{Cash}\ \text{Credit}\ \$2{,}800\\
\text{Notes Payable}\ \text{Credit}\ \$2{,}000
\]

For each account, indicate the side of the amount, write the posted line with date and reference J‑48, compute total debits and credits, and state the closing balance (debit or credit) after posting.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Ledger account & Permanent record that accumulates all debits and credits for one specific element of the accounting equation. \\
Debit & Entry recorded on the left side; increases assets and expenses, decreases liabilities, equity and revenue. \\
Credit & Entry recorded on the right side; increases liabilities, equity and revenue, decreases assets and expenses. \\
Posting & Systematic transfer of amounts from journal entries to the appropriate ledger accounts, preserving the double‑entry equality. \\
Balance & Net amount remaining after comparing total debits and total credits; shown as either a debit or credit balance and carried forward. \\
Normal balance rule & Guideline that determines whether an account's usual balance is a debit (assets, expenses) or a credit (liabilities, equity, revenue). \\
Trial balance & List of all ledger account balances at a point in time; used to check that total debits equal total credits before preparing financial statements. \\
Double‑entry & Fundamental accounting principle requiring that every transaction affect at least two accounts with equal total debits and credits. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{The Trial Balance}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item prepare a trial balance from the balances in the general ledger;
  \item recognise the main types of error that prevent a trial balance from balancing;
  \item apply systematic checks to locate and correct those errors;
  \item use a trial balance as the basis for preparing adjusting entries;
  \item understand the standard format of a trial balance and how to present it clearly.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Preparing a trial balance}

A \term{trial balance} is a statement that lists every account from the general ledger together with its ending balance. Debit balances are placed in a left‑hand column, credit balances in a right‑hand column. The purpose of the trial balance is to verify that the total of the debit column equals the total of the credit column, which confirms that the double‑entry bookkeeping rule (debits = credits) has been observed.

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step procedure}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Extract the account list.} Write down each account name exactly as it appears in the ledger.
  \item \textbf{Determine the balance type.} For each account decide whether the balance is a debit or a credit. Asset and expense accounts normally carry debit balances; liability, equity and revenue accounts normally carry credit balances.
  \item \textbf{Record the amount.} Write the ending amount in the appropriate column. Do not include any zero balances unless the instructor explicitly asks for them.
  \item \textbf{Add the columns.} Sum the debit column and the credit column separately, using a calculator or spreadsheet to avoid arithmetic mistakes.
  \item \textbf{Compare totals.} If the two totals are identical, the trial balance is said to be \term{balanced}. If they differ, an error exists somewhere in the ledger or in the trial‑balance preparation.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
The ledger at the end of the month shows the following balances:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Cash & 9,800 & \\
Accounts Receivable & 4,500 & \\
Prepaid Rent & 1,200 & \\
Equipment & 7,300 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 5,600\\
Bank Loan & & 6,200\\
Owner's Capital & & 10,000\\
\end{tabular}

Placing these amounts in trial‑balance format gives:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Cash & 9,800 & \\
Accounts Receivable & 4,500 & \\
Prepaid Rent & 1,200 & \\
Equipment & 7,300 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 5,600\\
Bank Loan & & 6,200\\
Owner's Capital & & 10,000\\
\midrule
Total & 22,800 & 21,800\\
\end{tabular}

The totals differ by \$1,000, indicating that an error is present. The next subsection explains how to find it. 
\end{example}

\subsection{Finding and correcting errors}

When the trial balance does not balance, the discrepancy originates from one or more of the following common error types.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Transposition error}: two adjacent digits are reversed (for example writing 63 instead of 36). The resulting difference is usually divisible by nine.
  \item \term{Slide error}: a digit is shifted one place value to the left or right (for example writing 1,200 instead of 12,000). The difference is typically a multiple of ten.
  \item \term{Omission}: a transaction is completely left out of the ledger, or an account is omitted from the trial‑balance list.
  \item \term{Commission}: the correct amount is entered on the wrong side of the trial balance (debit recorded as credit or vice‑versa).
  \item \term{Double entry error}: the same amount is posted to the same side of two accounts, breaking the debit‑credit equality.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Systematic checking technique}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Re‑add the columns.} Verify the arithmetic of the totals; a simple addition mistake can create a mismatch.
  \item \textbf{Check each ledger posting.} Ensure that every journal entry has been posted with equal debit and credit amounts.
  \item \textbf{Inspect individual account balances.} Re‑calculate the balance of each account from its subsidiary entries; a mis‑summed account will affect the trial balance.
  \item \textbf{Look for patterns in the difference.} If the discrepancy is divisible by nine, suspect a transposition; if it ends in zeroes, suspect a slide.
  \item \textbf{Cross‑reference the journal.} Confirm that every journal entry appears in the ledger and that no entry has been entered on the wrong side.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
In the trial balance of the previous example the debit total is \$22,800 and the credit total is \$21,800, a difference of \$1,000. The accountant notices that the Equipment account was recorded as \$7,300, but the original purchase invoice shows a cost of \$7,3000 (seven thousand three hundred dollars). The extra zero is a slide error. Correcting Equipment to \$7,3000 raises the debit total to \$29,800. At the same time the accountant discovers that a credit entry for Service Revenue of \$8,000 was omitted from the trial balance. Adding this credit brings the credit total to \$29,800, and the trial balance now balances.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
A small business prepares a trial balance and finds debits \$13,452 and credits \$13,452, so the totals match. Later the owner discovers that a payment of \$540 to a supplier was recorded as \$450 (a transposition). Although the trial balance still balances, the error will affect the financial statements. This example shows that a balanced trial balance does not guarantee that every individual posting is correct; it only confirms that total debits equal total credits.
\end{example}

\subsection{Using the trial balance for adjusting entries}

After the trial balance is prepared, accountants often need to make \term{adjusting entries} before the financial statements are finalized. Adjusting entries correct amounts that have been accrued, prepaid, or otherwise not yet reflected in the ledger. The trial balance provides the starting point for this process because it shows the current balances of all accounts.

\paragraph{Typical adjusting entries}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Accrued expenses: record an expense that has been incurred but not yet paid.
  \item Prepaid expenses: allocate a portion of a prepaid asset to expense for the period.
  \item Accrued revenue: recognise revenue that has been earned but not yet received.
  \item Depreciation: allocate the cost of a long‑term asset over its useful life.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Procedure}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Review the trial balance to identify accounts that require adjustment.
  \item Determine the correct amount for each adjustment based on supporting documents.
  \item Record the adjusting entry in the journal, ensuring debits equal credits.
  \item Post the adjusting entry to the appropriate ledger accounts, updating their balances.
  \item Prepare an adjusted trial balance by repeating the trial‑balance steps with the new balances.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
The trial balance shows Prepaid Rent of \$1,200. The rent covers a six‑month period, and two months have elapsed. The accountant calculates the monthly rent expense as \$200. An adjusting entry is required to move \$400 from Prepaid Rent to Rent Expense. The journal entry is:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Debit & Rent Expense & 400\\
Credit & Prepaid Rent & 400\\
\end{tabular}

Posting this entry reduces Prepaid Rent to \$800 and increases Rent Expense to \$400. The adjusted trial balance now reflects the correct expense for the period. 
\end{example}

\subsection{Standard format of a trial balance}

A trial balance is normally presented in a simple two‑column layout. The left column lists account names, the middle column records debit amounts, and the right column records credit amounts. A horizontal line separates the list of accounts from the totals, and a second line may be used to show the difference if the totals do not match. Consistent alignment makes it easy to scan for missing entries or misplaced figures.

\paragraph{Key formatting points}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Use the same order of accounts as in the ledger, typically assets first, then liabilities, equity, revenue and expenses.
  \item Align numbers by decimal point to aid visual comparison.
  \item Include a clear label for the total row and, if necessary, a separate row showing the difference.
  \item Keep the document tidy; blank lines or extra spaces can cause mis‑reading.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A correctly formatted trial balance might appear as follows:

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Cash & 5,000 & \\
Inventory & 3,200 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 2,800\\
Service Revenue & & 4,500\\
Rent Expense & 1,200 & \\
\midrule
Total & 9,400 & 7,300\\
Difference & 2,100 & \\
\end{tabular}

The difference row immediately signals that the trial balance is not balanced, prompting the error‑finding process described earlier. 
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Use the following ledger balances to prepare a trial balance. If the totals do not match, identify the error type and correct it. Then indicate one adjusting entry that would be required before final statements.

\begin{tabular}{lrr}
Account & Debit & Credit\\
Bank & 6,400 & \\
Inventory & 3,500 & \\
Furniture & 2,200 & \\
Accounts Payable & & 4,800\\
Service Revenue & & 9,300\\
\end{tabular}
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Trial balance & List of all ledger account balances with separate debit and credit columns; totals should be equal. \\
Transposition error & Digits are reversed, producing a small difference often divisible by nine. \\
Slide error & A digit is moved one place value, creating a difference that is a multiple of ten. \\
Omission & Failure to record a transaction in the ledger or to include an account in the trial balance. \\
Commission & Correct amount placed on the wrong side of the trial balance (debit instead of credit or vice‑versa). \\
Double‑entry error & Posting the same amount to the same side of two accounts, breaking the debit‑credit equality. \\
Adjusting entry & Journal entry made after the trial balance to correct accrued, prepaid, or other timing differences. \\
Standard format & Conventional two‑column layout with accounts, debit amounts, credit amounts, totals and optional difference line. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Adjusting Entries}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{accruals}, \term{prepayments}, \term{depreciation} and \term{bad debts};
  \item explain why each type of adjusting entry is required at period end;
  \item prepare adjusting journal entries for accrued revenues, accrued expenses, prepaid expenses, unearned revenues, depreciation (straight‑line and declining‑balance) and bad‑debt expense;
  \item recognise common errors such as forgetting to reverse a prepayment, using an incorrect useful‑life estimate, or mis‑classifying a write‑off;
  \item relate each adjustment to a realistic business situation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Accruals}
\term{Accruals} record economic events that have occurred but for which cash has not yet moved. They ensure that the income statement reflects the period in which the revenue was earned or the expense was incurred, satisfying the matching principle.

\subsubsection{Accrued revenue}
When a service is delivered before the customer pays, the revenue belongs to the period of performance. The adjusting entry creates a receivable and recognises revenue.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the amount earned but not yet billed.
  \item Debit \term{Accounts receivable} for that amount.
  \item Credit the appropriate revenue account.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Recording the cash receipt as revenue in the period it is received, which understates current‑period earnings.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A software firm completes a custom module on 31 March and invoices the client on 10 April. The module's price of \$4,500 must be recognised in March.

\begin{example}
A landscaping firm finished a \$2,700 job on 30 June. The client will pay on 15 July. The adjusting entry on 30 June is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Accounts receivable } & \$2{,}700 \\
\text{Credit Service revenue } & \$2{,}700
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Accrued expense}
Expenses that have been incurred but not yet paid are recorded to match them with the revenue they helped generate.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Determine the amount of expense incurred.
  \item Debit the relevant expense account.
  \item Credit a payable (e.g., \term{Wages payable}, \term{Interest payable}).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Omitting the payable and leaving the expense unrecorded, which overstates profit.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A retailer receives a monthly electricity bill on the first day of the next month. The consumption for the current month must be accrued.

\begin{example}
Interest on a bank loan for September totals \$350, payable on 5 October. The adjusting entry on 30 September is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Interest expense } & \$350 \\
\text{Credit Interest payable } & \$350
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Prepayments}
\term{Prepayments} (or deferrals) arise when cash is exchanged before the related economic benefit is received. The initial entry creates an asset (prepaid expense) or a liability (unearned revenue). At period end, part of that asset or liability is transferred to the income statement.

\subsubsection{Prepaid expense}
A payment for a future service is recorded as an asset and expensed over the benefit period.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Record the cash outflow as \term{Prepaid expense}.
  \item At each period‑end, calculate the portion that has been used.
  \item Debit the appropriate expense account and credit \term{Prepaid expense}.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Forgetting to reduce the prepaid balance, leaving assets overstated.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A law firm pays an annual professional‑insurance premium of \$12,000 on 1 January. Each month \$1,000 of insurance expense must be recognised.

\begin{example}
Insurance premium paid on 1 January for the year: \$12,000. The monthly adjusting entry on 31 January is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Insurance expense } & \$1{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Prepaid insurance } & \$1{,}000
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Unearned revenue}
Cash received before a service is performed creates a liability. As the service is delivered, the liability is reduced and revenue is recognised.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Record cash receipt as \term{Unearned revenue}.
  \item At each period‑end, determine the portion of the service that has been performed.
  \item Debit \term{Unearned revenue} and credit the appropriate revenue account.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Recognising the full amount as revenue immediately, which inflates current profit.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A magazine publisher receives \$1,200 on 1 July for a twelve‑month subscription. Each month \$100 of revenue is earned.

\begin{example}
Subscription received on 1 July for \$1,200. The adjusting entry on 31 July is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Unearned revenue } & \$100 \\
\text{Credit Subscription revenue } & \$100
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Depreciation}
Depreciation allocates the historical cost of a tangible asset over its expected useful life. The asset remains on the balance sheet at cost; accumulated depreciation, a contra‑asset, records the total expense taken to date.

\subsubsection{Straight‑line method}
The simplest approach spreads cost evenly over the useful life.

\[
\text{Depreciation expense} = \frac{\text{Cost} - \text{Residual value}}{\text{Useful life (periods)}}
\]

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Determine cost, residual value and useful life.
  \item Compute the periodic depreciation amount.
  \item Debit \term{Depreciation expense} and credit \term{Accumulated depreciation}.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Ignoring residual value, which overstates expense.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A delivery truck costing \$45,000 with a residual value of \$5,000 is expected to be used for 10 years. Annual depreciation is \$4,000.

\begin{example}
A computer system costs \$8,000, residual value \$800, useful life 4 years. Annual depreciation:
\[
\frac{8{,}000 - 800}{4} = \$1{,}800
\]
Adjusting entry at year‑end:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Depreciation expense } & \$1{,}800 \\
\text{Credit Accumulated depreciation -- Computer } & \$1{,}800
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Declining‑balance method}
An accelerated method that applies a constant rate to the beginning‑of‑period book value.

\[
\text{Depreciation expense} = \text{Book value at start} \times \text{Declining rate}
\]

The rate is often double the straight‑line rate (double‑declining).

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Compute straight‑line rate = 1 / useful life.
  \item Multiply by the acceleration factor (e.g., 2) to obtain the declining rate.
  \item Apply the rate to the beginning book value each period.
  \item Record the entry as for straight‑line.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Continuing to depreciate below residual value; the final year must be adjusted to stop at residual value.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} High‑tech equipment loses value quickly, so firms often use double‑declining to match the rapid loss of economic benefit.

\begin{example}
A machine costs \$22,000, residual value \$2,000, useful life 5 years. Double‑declining rate = 2 × (1/5) = 40\%. First‑year depreciation:
\[
22{,}000 \times 0.40 = \$8{,}800
\]
Adjusting entry:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Depreciation expense } & \$8{,}800 \\
\text{Credit Accumulated depreciation -- Machine } & \$8{,}800
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Bad Debts}
When some accounts receivable are expected to be uncollectible, an expense must be recognised. The allowance method estimates the amount and records a contra‑asset, \term{Allowance for doubtful accounts}.

\subsubsection{Estimating bad‑debt expense}
The estimate can be based on a percentage of credit sales, an aging of receivables, or historical loss rates.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step (percentage‑of‑sales method):}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Determine total credit sales for the period.
  \item Apply the estimated uncollectible percentage.
  \item Debit \term{Bad‑debt expense} and credit \term{Allowance for doubtful accounts}.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Forgetting to adjust the allowance when the estimate changes, leading to an inaccurate net receivables figure.

\textbf{Real‑life context:} A retailer with \$120,000 of credit sales expects 1.5\% will be uncollectible, resulting in a \$1,800 bad‑debt expense.

\begin{example}
Credit sales for the month are \$75,000; management expects 2.5\% will be uncollectible.
\[
\text{Bad‑debt expense} = 0.025 \times 75{,}000 = \$1{,}875
\]
Adjusting entry:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Bad‑debt expense } & \$1{,}875 \\
\text{Credit Allowance for doubtful accounts } & \$1{,}875
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Writing off a specific account}
When a particular receivable is identified as unrecoverable, the allowance is reduced and the receivable is removed.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step:}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Debit \term{Allowance for doubtful accounts}.
  \item Credit \term{Accounts receivable}.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistake:} Directly crediting \term{Accounts receivable} without using the allowance, which bypasses the estimation process.

\begin{example}
Customer A's account of \$2,300 is deemed uncollectible. The write‑off entry is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Debit Allowance for doubtful accounts } & \$2{,}300 \\
\text{Credit Accounts receivable } & \$2{,}300
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Key test}
\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each situation decide which adjusting entry type is required and write the journal entry:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A consulting firm performed services worth \$3,600 on 31 December; payment is due 8 January.
  \item Rent of \$9,600 paid on 1 August for a twelve‑month lease; adjust for August.
  \item A machine purchased for \$22,000, residual value \$2,000, useful life 5 years; record year‑end straight‑line depreciation.
  \item Credit sales of \$60,000 with an estimated 4\% uncollectible; record bad‑debt expense.
\end{enumerate}
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Accrual & Revenue earned or expense incurred before cash is received or paid; recorded via adjusting entries. \\
Prepayment & Cash paid or received before the related economic benefit is earned or incurred; initially recorded as an asset or liability and expensed/recognised over time. \\
Depreciation & Systematic allocation of a tangible asset's cost over its useful life; recognised as expense and accumulated depreciation. \\
Allowance for doubtful accounts & Contra‑asset that represents the estimated portion of receivables expected to be uncollectible. \\
Bad‑debt expense & Expense recognised for estimated uncollectible accounts receivable, using the allowance method. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{The Income Statement}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{revenues} and describe the criteria for recognising them under accrual accounting;
  \item explain the different categories of \term{expenses}, when each is recorded, and why classification matters;
  \item calculate \term{gross profit}, interpret its margin, and distinguish it from other profit measures;
  \item calculate \term{net profit}, explain its role in performance analysis, and identify typical sources of error;
  \item compare gross profit and net profit to illustrate how each reflects a different layer of a firm's cost structure.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Revenues}

\term{Revenues} are the inflows of economic benefits that arise from the ordinary activities of an entity during a reporting period. They represent the amount earned from selling goods, providing services, or other primary operations. Under the accrual basis, revenue is recognised when two conditions are met:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The entity has satisfied a performance obligation, meaning the customer has obtained control of the promised good or service.
  \item The amount of consideration can be measured reliably.
\end{enumerate}

Control transfers when the buyer can direct the use of, and obtain substantially all of the remaining benefits from, the asset. This may occur at a point in time (e.g., delivery of a product) or over time (e.g., a long‑term construction contract). The reliable measurement requirement means the price must be fixed or determinable and collectibility reasonably assured.

\textbf{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Recording cash advances as revenue before the related performance obligation is fulfilled.
  \item Including sales that are later cancelled, returned, or subject to significant discounts without adjusting the figure.
  \item Ignoring the distinction between revenue from core operations and ancillary income (e.g., interest earned), which should be presented separately.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A publishing house signs a contract on 1 January to deliver a quarterly magazine for \$9{,}000. The first issue is mailed on 15 January, and the customer receives it immediately. The performance obligation is satisfied on delivery, so the revenue for the quarter is recognised as \$9{,}000, even though the customer will pay on 30 March.
\end{example}

\subsection{Expenses}

\term{Expenses} are the outflows or de‑creasings of economic benefits that result from the entity's efforts to generate revenue. They are recorded when the related benefit is consumed, not necessarily when cash is paid. Expenses are grouped to aid analysis and to comply with accounting standards.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cost of goods sold (COGS)} -- direct costs of producing the goods sold or services delivered, such as raw materials, direct labour, and manufacturing overhead directly attributable to production.
  \item \textbf{Operating expenses} -- costs incurred to run the business that are not directly tied to production. These include selling expenses (advertising, commissions), general and administrative expenses (salaries of office staff, rent, utilities), and depreciation of non‑production assets.
  \item \textbf{Non‑operating expenses} -- items that are not part of ordinary operations, for example interest expense, loss on disposal of assets, or foreign exchange losses.
  \item \textbf{Tax expense} -- income taxes payable for the period, calculated on profit before tax.
\end{itemize}

The timing of expense recognition follows the matching principle: expenses are matched with the revenues they help generate. For instance, depreciation of a machine is spread over its useful life because the machine contributes to production over many periods.

\textbf{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Capitalising costs that should be expensed, such as routine maintenance, which inflates assets and understates current period expenses.
  \item Failing to accrue for incurred but unpaid items (e.g., wages earned by staff at month‑end), leading to an understatement of expenses and an overstatement of profit.
  \item Double‑counting an expense, for example recording depreciation both as an expense line and again as a reduction of the asset without proper adjustment.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A software development firm incurs the following costs in a month: programmer salaries \$12{,}000, cloud‑hosting fees \$1{,}500, office rent \$2{,}000, depreciation of office equipment \$800, and interest on a line of credit \$200. The expense breakdown is:
\[
\text{COGS} = \$12{,}000 \quad
\text{Operating expenses} = \$1{,}500 + \$2{,}000 + \$800 = \$4{,}300 \quad
\text{Non‑operating expense} = \$200
\]
Total expenses for the month equal \$12{,}000 + \$4{,}300 + \$200 = \$16{,}500.
\end{example}

\subsection{Gross profit}

\term{Gross profit} measures the profitability of a company's core production or service activities before the impact of operating and non‑operating costs. It isolates the relationship between sales and the direct cost of delivering those sales.

\[
\text{Gross profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{Cost of goods sold}
\]

\textbf{Interpretation}  
A higher gross profit margin (\(\frac{\text{Gross profit}}{\text{Revenues}}\)) suggests effective control of production costs, strong pricing power, or a favourable product mix. Conversely, a low margin may indicate high material costs, inefficient production, or intense price competition.

\textbf{Comparison with other profit measures}  
Gross profit does not consider selling, administrative, financing, or tax costs. Therefore it cannot be used alone to assess overall profitability, but it is valuable for benchmarking operational efficiency across firms in the same industry.

\textbf{Common mistake}  
Using total expenses instead of COGS in the formula. This would produce a figure closer to net profit and obscure the specific performance of core operations.

\begin{example}
A bakery reports quarterly revenue of \$85{,}000. The cost of flour, sugar, butter, and direct labour that went into the baked goods sold (COGS) is \$45{,}000. Gross profit is:
\[
\$85{,}000 - \$45{,}000 = \$40{,}000.
\]
The gross profit margin is \(\frac{\$40{,}000}{\$85{,}000} \approx 47\%\), indicating that nearly half of each sales dollar remains after covering direct production costs.
\end{example}

\subsection{Net profit}

\term{Net profit}, also called net income or profit after tax, is the residual amount after all expenses---including COGS, operating expenses, non‑operating expenses, interest, and taxes---have been deducted from revenues.

\[
\text{Net profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{All expenses}
\]

\textbf{Role in analysis}  
Net profit is the bottom‑line figure used to calculate earnings per share, return on equity, and many other performance ratios. It reflects the firm's ability to generate returns for shareholders after meeting all obligations.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step calculation}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start with total revenues.
  \item Subtract COGS to obtain gross profit.
  \item Subtract operating expenses to get operating profit (or EBIT).
  \item Subtract non‑operating expenses such as interest to obtain profit before tax.
  \item Subtract tax expense to arrive at net profit.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Omitting tax expense, which can dramatically inflate the reported profit.
  \item Forgetting to include all components of COGS, especially indirect manufacturing overhead.
  \item Double‑counting depreciation, either by recording it as an expense and also reducing the asset value without a corresponding expense entry.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
Continuing the bakery example, assume the same quarter incurs operating expenses of \$20{,}000 (staff wages, utilities, rent), interest expense of \$1{,}000, and tax expense of \$4{,}500. Total expenses are:
\[
\text{COGS} (\$45{,}000) + \text{Operating} (\$20{,}000) + \text{Interest} (\$1{,}000) + \text{Tax} (\$4{,}500) = \$70{,}500.
\]
Net profit equals:
\[
\$85{,}000 - \$70{,}500 = \$14{,}500.
\]
Thus the bakery retains \$14{,}500 for reinvestment or distribution to owners.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} A consumer‑electronics company reports the following for the year: revenue \$1{,}200{,}000; cost of goods sold \$720{,}000; selling and administrative expenses \$180{,}000; interest expense \$30{,}000; tax expense \$84{,}000. Compute its gross profit and net profit.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Revenues & Inflows earned from the entity's primary business activities, recognised when performance obligations are satisfied and the amount is measurable. \\
Expenses & Outflows or de‑creasings of economic benefits incurred to generate revenues; classified as COGS, operating, non‑operating, and tax expenses. \\
Gross profit & Difference between revenues and cost of goods sold; shows profitability of core operations before other costs. \\
Net profit & Revenues minus all expenses, including operating, non‑operating, interest, and tax items; the final profit figure used for overall performance assessment. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

