\section{The Balance Sheet}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{assets}, \term{liabilities} and \term{equity} with detailed explanations;
  \item distinguish \term{current} from \term{non‑current} items in each of the three sections and explain why the distinction matters;
  \item apply the accounting equation step‑by‑step and verify that it always balances;
  \item recognise typical classification errors, explain why they are wrong and correct them;
  \item read a balance sheet of a real company, interpret the numbers and relate them to the firm's operating and financing decisions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Assets}

\term{Assets} are economic resources that a business owns or controls and expects to generate future benefits. The benefits can be cash inflows, cost reductions, or the ability to produce goods and services. Assets are placed on the left side (or top) of the balance sheet because they represent what the entity possesses.

\paragraph{Two dimensions of classification}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current assets}: resources that will be turned into cash, sold, or consumed within the normal operating cycle---normally twelve months. Typical current assets are cash, cash equivalents, short‑term investments, accounts receivable, inventory, and prepaid expenses. The test is purely temporal: if the expected conversion or consumption occurs within one year, the item is current.
  \item \term{Non‑current assets}: resources that provide economic benefits for more than twelve months. This group includes property, plant and equipment (PPE), intangible assets (patents, trademarks, goodwill), long‑term investments, and deferred tax assets. Non‑current assets are usually depreciated (tangible) or amortised (intangible) to spread their cost over the period they generate benefits.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step classification}
1. Identify the nature of the resource (cash, inventory, equipment, licence, etc.).\\
2. Estimate the period over which the resource will be used, sold or converted into cash.\\
3. If the period is $\leq$ 12 months, record the item under \term{current assets}; otherwise, place it under \term{non‑current assets}.

\paragraph{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Classifying a long‑term investment that will be held for several years as a current asset because the market price can be quoted daily. The correct test is the intention to sell within a year, not marketability.
  \item Recording a prepaid expense (e.g., insurance paid for twelve months) as a non‑current asset. Prepaid expenses are current because the benefit will be consumed within the year.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A mid‑size consulting firm reports the following at year‑end:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cash in bank: \$48,000 (current);
  \item Accounts receivable: \$72,000, expected collection in 60 days (current);
  \item Office lease deposit: \$10,000, refundable at the end of a three‑year lease (non‑current);
  \item Computer hardware: \$30,000 with a useful life of five years (non‑current);
  \item Software licence (annual renewal): \$5,000 (current);
  \item Patent on a proprietary methodology: \$120,000, legal protection for 15 years (non‑current).
\end{itemize}
The balance sheet lists cash, receivables and the licence under current assets, while the lease deposit, hardware and patent appear in the non‑current‑asset section.
\end{example}

\subsection{Liabilities}

\term{Liabilities} are present obligations of the entity arising from past events, the settlement of which is expected to require an outflow of resources (usually cash). They are shown on the right side (or bottom) of the balance sheet because they represent claims against the assets.

\paragraph{Timing classification}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current liabilities}: obligations that must be settled within twelve months. Examples include accounts payable, short‑term bank borrowings, the current portion of long‑term debt, accrued wages, taxes payable, and the dividend payable that will be distributed within the next year.
  \item \term{Non‑current liabilities}: obligations whose settlement is due after twelve months. Typical items are long‑term bank loans, bonds payable, lease liabilities with terms longer than one year, and deferred tax liabilities.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step classification}
1. Identify the nature of the obligation (supplier invoice, loan agreement, lease).\\
2. Determine the next payment date or the date when the obligation must be settled.\\
3. If settlement is required within 12 months, classify as \term{current liability}; otherwise, classify as \term{non‑current liability}.

\paragraph{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Including the entire balance of a ten‑year loan in current liabilities. Only the portion that must be repaid in the next twelve months belongs to current liabilities; the rest stays in non‑current liabilities.
  \item Misclassifying accrued expenses that will be paid in six months as non‑current because they are not linked to a formal contract. The timing test overrides the form of the agreement.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A food‑processing company presents the following liabilities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Accounts payable to suppliers: \$45,000 (current);
  \item Short‑term bank overdraft: \$12,000 (current);
  \item Long‑term loan: \$200,000, with \$35,000 due in the next 12 months (current portion) and the remaining \$165,000 due thereafter (non‑current);
  \item Lease liability for a warehouse: \$80,000, with payments extending over eight years (non‑current);
  \item Deferred tax liability: \$22,000 (non‑current).
\end{itemize}
The balance sheet shows the \$35,000 as part of current liabilities and the \$165,000 together with the lease and tax items as non‑current liabilities.
\end{example}

\subsection{Equity}

\term{Equity} (also called owners' equity or shareholders' equity) represents the residual interest in the assets after all liabilities have been deducted. In formula form:

\[
\text{Equity} = \text{Assets} - \text{Liabilities}.
\]

Equity is displayed on the right side of the balance sheet, below liabilities, because it is the claim of the owners on what remains after creditors have been satisfied.

\paragraph{Main components}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Share capital}: cash or other assets contributed by owners in exchange for shares. It may be divided into common and preferred share capital.
  \item \term{Retained earnings}: cumulative net profit that has been retained in the business rather than distributed as dividends. Retained earnings increase with profit and decrease with losses or dividend payments.
  \item \term{Other reserves}: items such as revaluation surplus, foreign‑currency translation reserve, or treasury shares (shares repurchased by the company). These arise from specific accounting rules and are less common in introductory examples.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step calculation of total equity}
1. Add together all asset values to obtain \textit{total assets}.\\
2. Add together all liability values to obtain \textit{total liabilities}.\\
3. Subtract total liabilities from total assets. The result is \textit{total equity}.\\
4. Allocate the total equity to its components (share capital, retained earnings, other reserves) according to the company's records.

\paragraph{Common mistakes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Treating dividends paid during the year as an expense that reduces profit and then also subtracting the same amount from equity. Dividends are a distribution of equity; they reduce retained earnings directly, not profit.
  \item Forgetting to include treasury shares as a negative component of equity, which leads to an overstated equity figure.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A technology start‑up reports total assets of \$420,000 and total liabilities of \$260,000. Equity therefore equals \$160,000. The equity section is broken down as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Share capital: \$100,000;
  \item Retained earnings: \$55,000;
  \item Treasury shares (cost of repurchased shares): \$(5,000).
\end{itemize}
Adding the components (\$100,000 + \$55,000 -- \$5,000) gives the total equity of \$160,000, confirming the accounting equation.
\end{example}

\subsection{Current and Non‑Current Items Across the Three Sections}

The current/non‑current distinction is applied consistently to assets and liabilities. For equity, the distinction is rarely shown on the face of the balance sheet, but time‑related equity items (e.g., share‑based payment awards that vest over several years) are disclosed in the notes.

\paragraph{Why the distinction matters}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Liquidity analysis}: Comparing current assets with current liabilities yields the current ratio, a key indicator of short‑term solvency.
  \item \textbf{Solvency analysis}: The mix of non‑current assets and non‑current liabilities reveals the long‑term financing structure and the firm's capacity to generate future cash flows.
  \item \textbf{Capital intensity insight}: Investors look at the proportion of non‑current assets (e.g., factories, patents) relative to equity to assess how much capital is tied up in long‑term resources.
  \item \textbf{Regulatory compliance}: Accounting standards require separate presentation of current and non‑current items to improve comparability between firms.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Typical layout of a balance sheet}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Assets}
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Current assets (top)
      \item Non‑current assets (bottom)
    \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Liabilities}
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Current liabilities (top)
      \item Non‑current liabilities (bottom)
    \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Equity}
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Share capital
      \item Retained earnings
      \item Other reserves (if any)
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
Balance sheet of a regional supermarket chain (figures in \$):

\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Assets} & \\
\midrule
Current assets: & \\
\quad Cash & 18,000 \\
\quad Accounts receivable & 12,000 \\
\quad Inventory & 45,000 \\
\quad Prepaid rent (12 months) & 3,000 \\
Non‑current assets: & \\
\quad Store buildings & 250,000 \\
\quad Delivery trucks & 40,000 \\
\quad Franchise goodwill & 30,000 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Liabilities} & \\
\midrule
Current liabilities: & \\
\quad Accounts payable & 28,000 \\
\quad Short‑term bank loan & 7,000 \\
\quad Current portion of long‑term loan & 15,000 \\
Non‑current liabilities: & \\
\quad Mortgage on store buildings & 120,000 \\
\quad Long‑term loan (balance) & 50,000 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Equity} & \\
\midrule
Share capital & 100,000 \\
Retained earnings & 45,000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

Current assets total \$78,000; current liabilities total \$50,000, giving a current ratio of 1.56. Non‑current assets (\$320,000) are financed mainly by the mortgage (\$120,000) and equity (\$145,000), indicating a solid long‑term capital structure.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each line item on a balance sheet ask:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Does the item represent a resource owned or controlled by the entity? $\rightarrow$ Asset.
  \item Does the item represent an obligation to transfer resources? $\rightarrow$ Liability.
  \item Is the item the residual claim after liabilities are deducted? $\rightarrow$ Equity.
  \item Will the resource be realised or the obligation settled within twelve months? $\rightarrow$ Current; otherwise, Non‑current.
\end{enumerate}
After classification, verify that
\[
\text{Total Assets} = \text{Total Liabilities} + \text{Total Equity}.
\]
Any mismatch signals a recording error that must be investigated.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Classify each item as current or non‑current:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Cash on hand;
      \item A five‑year lease liability;
      \item Finished‑goods inventory;
      \item A patent with fifteen years of legal protection;
      \item The portion of a ten‑year bank loan that is due in the next six months.
    \end{enumerate}
  \item A company reports: total assets \$540,000; current liabilities \$80,000; non‑current liabilities \$190,000. Compute total equity and state whether the accounting equation balances.
  \item Identify the error in each statement and rewrite it correctly:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item ``The \$30,000 bank loan is listed as a current asset because it will be repaid within one year.''
      \item ``Retained earnings appear under current liabilities because the company expects to distribute them as dividends this year.''
    \end{enumerate}
  \item Using the balance sheet in the previous example, calculate the current ratio and the debt‑to‑equity ratio (total liabilities divided by total equity). Interpret both ratios briefly.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
Term & Meaning \\
\midrule
Assets & Resources owned or controlled that are expected to generate future economic benefits. \\
Current assets & Assets expected to be realised in cash, sold, or consumed within twelve months. \\
Non‑current assets & Assets providing benefits for more than twelve months; usually depreciated or amortised. \\
Liabilities & Present obligations to transfer economic resources to others. \\
Current liabilities & Obligations due for settlement within twelve months. \\
Non‑current liabilities & Obligations due after twelve months. \\
Equity & Residual interest in assets after deducting liabilities; owners' claim on the business. \\
Share capital & Money or other assets contributed by owners in exchange for ownership units (shares). \\
Retained earnings & Cumulative net profit retained in the business rather than paid out as dividends. \\
Current & Classification based on a twelve‑month horizon; indicates short‑term liquidity. \\
Non‑current & Classification based on a horizon longer than twelve months; indicates long‑term solvency. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

